<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<template xmlns="http://schemas.openehr.org/v1">
    <id>
        <value>lifestyle_medicine_encounter.v0</value>
    </id>
    <name>
        <value>Lifestyle Medicine Clinical Encounter</value>
    </name>
    <description>
        <original_author>
            <item>
                <key>name</key>
                <value>Ricardo Lourenco dos Santos</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>organisation</key>
                <value>Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto (FMUP)</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>email</key>
                <value>ricardolourencosantos@gmail.com</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>date</key>
                <value>2026-01-29</value>
            </item>
        </original_author>
        <lifecycle_state>in_development</lifecycle_state>
        <details>
            <purpose>To record a comprehensive lifestyle medicine clinical encounter that integrates wearable device data with clinical observations, enabling patient-clinician shared decision making based on objective biomarker trends.</purpose>
            <keywords>lifestyle medicine, clinical encounter, wearable data review, patient consultation, biomarker trends, shared decision making, ACLM, six pillars</keywords>
            <use>Use for recording a complete lifestyle medicine consultation including:
- Review of wearable-collected data (HRV, activity, sleep)
- Clinical vital signs measured during encounter
- Integration of patient-generated health data (PGHD)
- Lifestyle assessment across six pillars (ACLM)
- Goal setting and intervention planning
- Follow-up scheduling

Primary use cases:
1. Initial lifestyle medicine assessment
2. Follow-up consultations with trend review
3. Wearable data interpretation sessions
4. Lifestyle intervention progress tracking
5. Multi-disciplinary team reviews

Compatible with:
- ACLM (American College of Lifestyle Medicine) pillars
- WHO Physical Activity Guidelines
- DASH/Mediterranean diet assessments
- Sleep hygiene evaluations</use>
            <misuse>Not for:
- Emergency medical encounters
- Acute illness management
- Specialized cardiology consultations (use specific templates)</misuse>
            <copyright>CC BY-SA 4.0</copyright>
        </details>
        <other_details>
            <item>
                <key>licence</key>
                <value>Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>custodian_organisation</key>
                <value>FMUP - Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>references</key>
                <value>1. Rippe JM. Lifestyle Medicine. 3rd ed. CRC Press; 2019.
2. ACLM. Lifestyle Medicine Core Competencies. American College of Lifestyle Medicine; 2023.
3. WHO Guidelines on Physical Activity and Sedentary Behaviour. Geneva: WHO; 2020.
4. Bodai BI, et al. Lifestyle Medicine: A Brief Review of Its Dramatic Impact on Health and Survival. Perm J. 2018;22:17-025.</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>FHIR_mapping</key>
                <value>Maps to Encounter resource with extensions for lifestyle medicine context. References HRVObservation, PhysicalActivitySummary, SleepObservation profiles.</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>six_pillars</key>
                <value>ACLM Six Pillars: 1. Nutrition, 2. Physical Activity, 3. Sleep, 4. Stress Management, 5. Avoidance of Risky Substances, 6. Positive Social Connections</value>
            </item>
        </other_details>
    </description>
